[
  {
    "sha": "2757f4d4831df0346ddb6f0d252e3dacaeadad23",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOV1WsOtoAKDI3NTdmNGQ0ODMxZGYwMzQ2ZGRiNmYwZDI1MmUzZGFjYWVhZGFkMjM",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Quintin Smits",
        "email": "qd.smits@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-27T22:18:11Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Quintin Smits",
        "email": "qd.smits@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-27T22:18:11Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(tools): holder form shows the outline's largest diameter; a covered body is not read back smaller\n\nThe holder form's \"Largest diameter\" field held the body steel below the\nflange (the ER32 nut Ø50 of a BT40 ER32), while the drawing states the\nlargest diameter of the outline (the BT40 flange Ø63). The editable field\nis now \"Body diameter\"; a read-only \"Largest diameter\" row shows the\ndrawing's value.\n\nHolderShape::of read a shrink-fit body that the flange envelope covers\nback as the narrowest body that draws the outline (Ø41.5 for the Ø42 body\nof \"BT40 Shrink 20 mm\"). A longer gauge length then drew the body smaller\nthan the steel. `of` now refuses an outline that a wider body or a\nnarrower nose also draws, except a library chuck at its library gauge\nlength, which reads back as the library's steel. HolderShape::component\nrefuses to write a shape that does not read back as itself; the form's\ncheck and Apply use it.\n\nFixes #4455\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
